00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:44.819
This doesn't look like much.

00:00:44.819 --> 00:00:48.544
If you saw it on the street, you’d think,
"Yeah that's, that's a bit of gravel."

00:00:48.544 --> 00:00:56.847
But every day, tiny little things, just like this
are raining down on our planet from space.

00:00:56.847 --> 00:00:59.359
Each one is small, about a millimeter across.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:01.823
So when they land, we barely notice.

00:01:01.823 --> 00:01:05.955
But over the course of a year, each individual
piece that makes its way to Earth

00:01:05.955 --> 00:01:10.042
adds up to around 30,000 tons.

00:01:10.042 --> 00:01:15.360
That’s a lot of tons of stuff that are 
so tiny that they escapes our notice.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:21.872
But of course, here on journey to the microcosmos,
these are exactly the kinds of things

00:01:21.872 --> 00:01:23.701
that we like to look at.

00:01:23.701 --> 00:01:31.722
These are micrometeorites, just a few of millions
that pass through our atmosphere every single day.

00:01:31.722 --> 00:01:34.128
Most don’t survive the journey from space.

00:01:34.128 --> 00:01:36.870
They simply vaporize in our atmosphere.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:43.009
But those that do survive go through quite a transformation
as they soar through our planet’s atmosphere,

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:45.700
melting with the friction of their entrance.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:50.920
Depending on their speed and rotation and
chemical composition, their shapes warp and

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.335
recrystallize in new shapes.

00:01:53.335 --> 00:01:58.510
They may become more elongated, or perhaps
develop pointed shapes around their body.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.440
But from a distance, they could be anything.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:06.670
A piece of sand or a speck of dust… so,
if it seems difficult to find a micrometeorite,

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.910
that’s because it is.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.770
James, our master of microscopes, received
these samples from a friend of his, Scott

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:20.250
Peterson, who has been hunting micrometeorites
for some time, examining rooftops and their

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:24.709
relatively undisturbed environments to see
what he can find.

00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:28.690
And if you want to see how well you would
do as a micrometeorite hunter, here’s a

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.003
little test for your skills.

00:02:31.003 --> 00:02:35.080
Let’s say you’re looking at this array
of pearly looking bits and bobs.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.871
Do you think you can find micrometeorites
there?

00:02:37.871 --> 00:02:39.520
You can pause, take a moment.

00:02:40.503 --> 00:02:43.211
This is the micrometeorite.

00:02:43.211 --> 00:02:47.440
Alright so now that you’ve seen one, can you 
find them in the next few slides we’ll show you?

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.349
Again, just pause if you need 
a moment, take a look,

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:53.190
and we will show you the 
micrometeorites at the end.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:54.888
Let’s start with this.

00:02:54.888 --> 00:02:56.328
Then this.

00:02:56.328 --> 00:02:57.971
Then this.

00:02:57.971 --> 00:03:00.189
And last, this.

00:03:00.189 --> 00:03:03.031
So now that you’ve had a moment to look
at them all, let’s go through them to see

00:03:03.031 --> 00:03:04.572
how well you did.

00:03:04.572 --> 00:03:10.349
First, we have the micrometeorite here, which
looks similar to the other pearly round things,

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:12.922
except that it’s a little  less shiny.

00:03:12.922 --> 00:03:16.535
And then here, the micrometeorite was right
in the middle of everything.

00:03:16.535 --> 00:03:20.207
While here, it was off in the corner.

00:03:20.207 --> 00:03:25.125
And our last one seems practically impossible
to distinguish from everything else.

00:03:25.125 --> 00:03:30.090
Sorting out these micrometeorites feels a
bit like trying to navigate the microcosmos.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:36.476
There’s just so much stuff to sort through,
it requires patience and it requires knowledge.

00:03:36.476 --> 00:03:43.430
But at the end, the result is understanding that allows
us to identify and classify our universe so

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.680
that we can build some kind of framework to
understand it.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:51.880
So it is weird and cool that we just walk
around our world surrounded by these little

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.773
bits of the cosmos that got past our atmosphere.

00:03:54.773 --> 00:03:59.088
Sort of like it’s weird and cool that we
just walk around our world surrounded by

00:03:59.088 --> 00:04:02.142
microbes that we never see or think about.

00:04:02.142 --> 00:04:07.220
And for us, as people who talk about microbes
a lot, it’s hard to not spend some of this

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:12.970
time imagining what it would be like for our
microbes to make the opposite journey.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:19.150
To go from our world, past the atmosphere,
and back into whatever is beyond.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:25.311
Unfortunately, as talented as our master of
microscopes is, he has yet to go into space.

00:04:25.311 --> 00:04:29.522
So just know that the footage we’re showing
now are not actually microbes in space.

00:04:29.522 --> 00:04:36.187
Think of them more as a vibe, a background
for us to project onto as we consider the possibilities.

00:04:36.187 --> 00:04:39.680
Microbes are crafty, hardy creatures.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:44.530
In the billions of years that they have existed,
they’ve managed to adapt to countless environments

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.470
and survived monumental shifts in climate.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:52.787
But we also live on a planet that has so far
shown itself to be uniquely nurturing.

00:04:52.787 --> 00:04:54.689
What about everywhere else?

00:04:55.114 --> 00:04:58.062
Now we’re not talking about aliens, though of
course we could.

00:04:58.062 --> 00:05:03.771
There is a rover on Mars right now, seeking
out the possibility that life may have existed there.

00:05:03.771 --> 00:05:08.307
But we don’t need to consider aliens to
imagine microbes in space.

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:09.055
Because...

00:05:09.055 --> 00:05:10.840
there are microbes there.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:12.590
We brought them there.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:17.990
Because well, humans have gone to space,
and where we go, microbes go as well.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:21.811
In our cargo, our food, and of course, our
bodies.

00:05:22.604 --> 00:05:27.430
In 2020, researchers studying the International
Space Station found that after two decades

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:33.479
of humans living there, the space station
itself seems to have a microbiome of its own,

00:05:33.479 --> 00:05:39.770
made up of 55 microbial genera—an array
of bacteria and fungi and protists and other

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:43.717
creatures that are invisible roommates to
the astronauts.

00:05:43.717 --> 00:05:48.699
And if you’re painting an image of superpowered
microbes that have been mutated into something

00:05:48.699 --> 00:05:55.577
unrecognizable and dangerous by cosmic rays…fortunately,
NASA looked into it, and they found the microbes

00:05:55.577 --> 00:05:56.817
are not dangerous.

00:05:56.817 --> 00:06:02.319
Though there were some microbes, particularly
fungi, that have caused some damage to windows

00:06:02.319 --> 00:06:06.143
because of their unusual ability to grow on
metal.

00:06:06.143 --> 00:06:10.770
And even when humans aren't being sent to
space, our microbial marks can be left on

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:15.090
the things we do send to space,
which does pose some challenges.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:19.710
For example, the Perseverance rover is looking
for microbes on Mars, but it needs to make

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:26.580
sure that any signs of microbial life it sees
aren’t actually accidental terrestrial hitchhikers.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:31.930
So NASA had to build the spacecraft while
keeping it as clean as possible, ensuring

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:37.780
that the Mars 2020 Mission was launched with
10 times less bacteria on it than the amount

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:41.324
that live in a teaspoon of seawater.

00:06:41.324 --> 00:06:45.120
Because space might not kill those microbes
for us.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:51.030
The ones that can form spores or cysts can
use those adaptations out in space as well.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:56.310
So even with the vacuum and the radiation
and the massive shifts in temperatures, some

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:58.905
microbes can survive.

00:06:59.519 --> 00:07:06.608
ln 2020, a team of researchers created dried
aggregates of the UV radiation-resistant bacteria

00:07:06.608 --> 00:07:12.945
Deinococcus radiodurans and they put them 
on panels outside the International Space Station—

00:07:12.945 --> 00:07:16.440
a place where not much seems like it should
survive.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:23.440
But after three years, they found bacteria
in those pellets that survived, buried under

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.728
a surface layer that hadn’t survived but
was able to protect those underneath.

00:07:28.728 --> 00:07:34.000
There’s something about that—about the
way a mass of bacteria can be dried out and

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:41.940
placed in an environment so far from its origins—that
feels perfect as a meeting of the microcosmos

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.504
and the cosmos.

00:07:43.504 --> 00:07:49.459
Just as micrometeorites burn through our atmosphere
and blend with our planet, microbes can

00:07:49.459 --> 00:07:55.238
find their own way to become a part of the
world outside our planet.

